            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The harlot primarily symbolizes spiritual unfaithfulness, idolatry, and covenant apostasy, likened to marital infidelity. While this core meaning is widely agreed upon, the specific identity of the 'great harlot' in Revelation 17 is heavily debated, with imperial Rome being the dominant scholarly identification, and apostate Jerusalem, the papacy, or a generic world system as notable alternatives.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The harlot symbolizes a covenant city or people that is unfaithful to its Husband while retaining the outward form of the covenant. In Revelation, this specifically refers to apostate Jerusalem, not Rome or a generic world system.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Spiritual unfaithfulness and covenant apostasy.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A covenant city or people unfaithful to its Husband.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"EQUIVALENT\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book strictly applies the requirement of a prior covenant bond (a 'Husband') to the Harlot of Revelation, thereby excluding non-covenantal entities like pagan Rome or generic world systems, and limiting the referent to Jerusalem.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no contradiction in the core symbolic meaning. The book simply adopts the minority preterist identification (Jerusalem) acknowledged by the consensus, explicitly rejecting the dominant alternative (Rome).\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Both the book and the consensus agree that the harlot represents covenantal unfaithfulness. The book refines this by rigorously applying the 'covenant' requirement to the Revelation harlot, logically restricting the referent to Jerusalem and excluding the consensus's most prominent historical candidate, Rome.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Revelation 17:18 describes the Harlot as 'that great city, which reigneth 